= Introduction

== Purpose of this report

This report describes the technical performance of the regional water supply network in 2025. It covers raw water resources, treatment, distribution, leakage, energy use, asset condition, customer service, water quality, monitoring, health and safety, and the investment programme for the coming years. It is written for the operating board, the regulator and the customer panel, and it supports the annual performance return.

The figures in this report are taken from the operational database at the end of January. Where a figure is provisional, this is stated in the text. Values are rounded for presentation, so totals in tables may differ slightly from the sum of their parts.

== The network at a glance

The network supplies drinking water to about 412,000 properties through four surface water treatment works, nine groundwater sources, 61 service reservoirs and roughly 6,900 kilometres of mains. Average demand in 2025 was 118 megalitres per day, with a peak week demand of 141 megalitres per day in July. The network is divided into seven supply zones, each with its own control strategy and a set of district metered areas.

The largest sources are the impounding reservoirs in the upland catchments, which provide about two thirds of the water supplied. The groundwater sources supply the coastal and eastern zones, and are blended with surface water where nitrate levels require it. Bulk transfers between zones are possible through three strategic mains, which were used for planned outages and during the summer peak.

